/*
|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
| OpenUI backend handler — PHP / Laravel 13.x
|--------------------------------------------------------------------------
|
| Two files, both shown in this template:
|   1. routes/api.php           -> registers POST /api/chat
|   2. app/Http/Controllers/ChatController.php
|
| Setup (Laravel 13.x, PHP 8.3+; Laravel 13 supports PHP 8.3 to 8.5):
|   composer create-project laravel/laravel openui-backend
|   cd openui-backend
|   # API routes are opt-in on a fresh app; install the api route file once:
|   php artisan install:api      # creates routes/api.php, prefixes routes with /api
|   # drop your generated prompt next to the app:
|   #   php artisan ... -> place system-prompt.txt at storage/app/system-prompt.txt
|   php artisan serve            # serves on http://127.0.0.1:8000
|
| composer.json require block (minimum):
|   "require": {
|       "php": "^8.3",
|       "laravel/framework": "^13.0"
|   }
|   (Laravel already bundles guzzlehttp/guzzle, which backs the Http facade.
|    No extra dependency is needed to call OpenAI.)
|
| Wire protocol: the frontend expects OpenAI-compatible SSE at POST /api/chat
| (`data: {json}\n\n` lines terminated by `data: [DONE]`). We forward OpenAI's
| native SSE stream VERBATIM and flush per chunk. Pair with
| `streamProtocol={openAIAdapter()}` on the React side.
|
| Why response()->stream() and NOT response()->eventStream():
|   eventStream() re-frames every yield as a named SSE event and appends its own
|   `</stream>` sentinel — that rewrites the bytes and breaks openAIAdapter(),
|   which wants OpenAI's raw `data: {chunk}` frames and the literal
|   `data: [DONE]`. stream() lets us echo upstream bytes untouched.
|
| Env (.env):
|   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
|   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1     # override for OpenAI-compatible providers
|   OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.5
|   FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
*/

